WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.820
Good morning, Hank. It's Tuesday.

00:00:00.820 --> 00:00:04.480
So this is the Pantheon, the world's oldest building still in use,

00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:07.320
depending on your definition of "building" and "use."

00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:11.500
It was finished around 126 CE during the reign of the Roman emperor Hadrian,

00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:14.280
seen here (noseless) in his most famous statue,

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:16.680
which, by the way, was recently discovered to be a forgery.

00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:21.020
Hadrian's head was put on someone else's body 1700 years after his death.

00:00:21.020 --> 00:00:23.480
Right, so the Pantheon was originally a temple to the Roman gods,

00:00:23.480 --> 00:00:26.720
but it became a Christian church in 609, and it still is one.

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:30.360
For almost 1900 years, people have been coming to this place to worship—

00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:32.400
so long that the gods have changed.

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:35.040
When the Pantheon was built, there was no English language.

00:00:35.260 --> 00:00:41.100
Romans didn't eat pizza because tomatoes wouldn't come to Italy for another 1500 years.

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:44.440
If you wanted to get from Rome to Florence and you were phenomenally wealthy,

00:00:44.440 --> 00:00:48.860
it would take four days by carriage, at a cost of 350 denarii,

00:00:48.860 --> 00:00:52.280
approximately equal to a year's wages for a labourer.

00:00:52.280 --> 00:00:56.580
Today, it takes about three hours by car, or three and a half if you want to avoid the tolls.

00:00:56.580 --> 00:01:00.820
In short, human life was very different 1900 years ago,

00:01:00.820 --> 00:01:01.740
and yet...

00:01:01.740 --> 00:01:05.900
This is the Lascaux cave in southwestern France, which was discovered—

00:01:05.900 --> 00:01:09.340
or rediscovered—in 1940 by four teenagers and a dog named Robot.

00:01:09.340 --> 00:01:13.280
The cave is famous for its nearly two thousand prehistoric wall paintings,

00:01:13.280 --> 00:01:16.740
including extraordinary depictions of animals, some of them now extinct,

00:01:16.740 --> 00:01:19.300
as well as this famous person with a bird head.

00:01:19.300 --> 00:01:21.500
Now there's a lot we don't know about these paintings at Lascaux.

00:01:21.500 --> 00:01:24.220
We don't know, for instance, how many generations painted there. But

00:01:24.220 --> 00:01:28.320
we do know, from carbon dating and other dating methods, that the paintings are mostly

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:30.720
between fifteen and twenty thousand years old.

00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:34.420
350 kilometers away, a group of cave experts found another cave

00:01:34.420 --> 00:01:37.160
full of extraordinary paintings in 1996.

00:01:37.160 --> 00:01:39.280
This one came to be known as the Chauvet cave.

00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:41.620
And in many ways, these paintings are even more sophisticated

00:01:41.620 --> 00:01:44.200
than the ones from Lascaux. There's more action in them,

00:01:44.200 --> 00:01:48.000
like animals locking horns, or drawn so as to intimate motion.

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:50.700
And the Chauvet paintings are also arguably more detailed.

00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:55.160
But, the Chauvet paintings are much older than the Lascaux paintings.

00:01:55.160 --> 00:01:56.680
Like, much, much older.

00:01:56.680 --> 00:02:01.420
Most of these paintings were made between thirty-seven and thirty-three thousand years ago,

00:02:01.420 --> 00:02:04.400
although a few were created during a second period of activity

00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:06.040
thirty-one to twenty-eight thousand years ago.

00:02:06.040 --> 00:02:09.880
That means the historical distance between Chauvet and Lascaux

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:15.120
is approximately equal to the historical distance between Lascaux and NOW.

00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:17.900
And even more remarkably, the paintings in the Chauvet cave

00:02:17.900 --> 00:02:20.280
were created across thousands of years,

00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:25.060
meaning that that cave was almost certainly in use by the same community

00:02:25.060 --> 00:02:28.520
for longer than the Pantheon has been in use by us.

00:02:28.520 --> 00:02:30.860
We like to think that history moves in a line:

00:02:30.860 --> 00:02:35.300
from naive art to sophisticated art; from short, disease-ridden human lives

00:02:35.300 --> 00:02:36.760
to long and healthy ones;

00:02:36.760 --> 00:02:39.700
from poverty to abundance; from ignorance to knowledge.

00:02:39.700 --> 00:02:42.940
But the idea that history means progress is very new.

00:02:42.940 --> 00:02:46.540
Like, almost all of the 250,000-year history of humans

00:02:46.540 --> 00:02:49.580
looks more like a sine curve than an ascending line.

00:02:49.580 --> 00:02:54.060
And whatever progress we've made toward justice or equality or shared prosperity

00:02:54.060 --> 00:02:56.360
is fragile because it's new.

00:02:56.360 --> 00:02:58.980
It's easy to forget that because the new is all around us—

00:02:58.980 --> 00:03:02.740
from broadband Internet to Italian tomatoes to even the Pantheon.

00:03:02.740 --> 00:03:06.460
But all of that occurred in the last one percent of human history,

00:03:06.460 --> 00:03:10.040
which is maybe a reminder that what a lot of what we think of as inevitable

00:03:10.040 --> 00:03:13.080
or natural about humanness really isn't.

00:03:13.080 --> 00:03:17.940
Inequality of wealth isn't natural, nor is anthropogenic climate change or political polarization.

00:03:17.940 --> 00:03:21.880
On the other hand, literacy also isn't natural, nor is freedom of speech,

00:03:21.880 --> 00:03:25.120
nor is it natural for Rome to be three hours from Florence.

00:03:25.120 --> 00:03:30.420
These conditions result from choices we've made collectively. Every day, we're choosing what to value,

00:03:30.420 --> 00:03:32.400
what to worship, what to paint.

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:35.600
And through the haze of history, the humans of the future will know us

00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:39.120
by the choices that we are making together right now.

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:41.040
Hank, I'll see you on Friday.

